    <div class="text-body text-sm sm:text-[15px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>One important limit first: PasteDownloadApp converts <strong>format</strong>. It does not resize an image's pixel dimensions. Say you need a 512&times;512 icon, but your source photo is 4000&times;3000. This tool will give you a 4000&times;3000 file in whatever format you choose, not a resized one. What it is genuinely useful for is picking the right format and understanding transparency. That is where most people actually go wrong. Here is what each use case needs, backed by a real conversion of an actual 512&times;512 app icon.</p>

        <h2>Favicons and App Icons: Transparency Is the Whole Point</h2>

        <p>A favicon or app icon almost always needs a transparent background. That way it sits cleanly on a browser tab or home screen, regardless of theme. I ran this site's own 512&times;512 app icon (22.3 KB PNG with an alpha channel) through the conversion pipeline to see what each format does to it:</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
                <thead class="bg-canvas-soft text-ink">
                    <tr>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Format</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Size</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Transparency</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">PNG (source)</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">22.3 KB</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Preserved</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">JPEG</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">16.2 KB</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Lost, filled with solid white</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-ink">WebP</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">5.7 KB</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-3">Preserved</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <p>JPEG's smaller size is irrelevant here. It silently destroys the transparent background, replacing it with solid white. That looks broken the moment the icon sits on anything other than a white surface. For a favicon or app icon, the real choice is between PNG and WebP. PNG is the safest, most universally supported option. WebP came out 76% smaller in this test, with transparency intact, though not every icon-generation tool accepts WebP input. If you are working from an SVG source instead of a PNG, the rasterization quirks in <a href="/blog/svg-ico-conversion-quirks/">converting SVG and ICO files</a> apply here too.</p>

        <h2>Social Media Posts: JPEG or WebP, Rarely PNG</h2>

        <p>Social platforms recompress every image you upload, regardless of what you send them. So arriving with the smallest reasonable file saves upload time, without any visible quality cost. Unless the image needs transparency (a logo overlay, for instance), JPEG or WebP at PasteDownloadApp's default 92% quality is the right choice. Skip PNG for ordinary photos here. As shown in <a href="/blog/why-png-reencode-bigger/">why re-saving a PNG can make the file bigger</a>, re-encoding a photo as PNG through a browser tool typically produces a larger file. It ends up bigger than the JPEG or WebP alternative, with no quality benefit for content that started as a photograph.</p>

        <h2>Resumes: PDF Is Usually Right, But if It Must Be an Image</h2>

        <p>Most applicant tracking systems expect a PDF, not an image. That is the better target if you have a choice. Sometimes a system specifically asks for an image, a headshot upload, for example. In that case, PNG is the safer default if the image has any text or a logo with sharp edges. JPEG artifacts around text are a genuine reliability risk when a document might be read closely or printed. For a plain photograph with no text, JPEG at high quality keeps the file small without a visible downside.</p>

